{"text": "High-Field Conduction in Films of Mylar and Teflon\nby A. C. LILLY, JR., and JOHN R. McDOWELL, Journal of Applied\u2019 Physics, 39, 141 (1968).\n\nIN an investigation of the mechanism of charge storage\nin dielectrics. a study was made of the steady-state eles-\ntrical conduction of polymer films. These included\npolyethylene terephthalate (as Mylar film type A)\nand, to a lesser extent, polytetratluoroethylene (as\nTeflon film).\nSelected specimens were cut from films ranging in\nthickness from one to 10 mils. The sample and elec-\ntrodes, which were stainless stee! disks with guard rings,\nwere enclosed in a grounded copper screen\u2019 box in an\n\u2018oven whose temperature was controlled to = 0.5\u00b0 C\nby a proportional controller. A well-regulated power\nsupply was used to establish a potential difference\nacross the sample over the range of one to 10* V.\nSample current was measured with a vibrating reed\nelectrometer. Both the electrode and guard-ring cur-\nrents were measured, The sensitivity of the system was\nsuch that currents as low as 10 A could be measured.\nBoth one-minute and steady-state currents were\nmeasured, with the one-minute values generally being\none to two orders of magnitude higher than the steady\nstate values, but having the same temperature gradient.\nOnly the steady-state values. were used to: test con\nduction theories. At higher temperatures, the time for\nthe sample to reach steady state was a few minutes:\nthis time increased to as long as one to two weeks for\ntemperatures below 100\u00b0 C.\nThe current-voltage (I-V) behavior did not fit a\ncurve of the form I =I, sinh aV, as previously thought\nfor Mylar. The current varied with voltage as exp\n(aVy\"2), and its temperature behavior was consistent\nwith an. activation process. giving an activation enersy\nfor conduction essentially independent of sample thick-\nress except at low fields. At lower fields there seemed\nto be a gradual transition between Ohmic slore